{
  "$comment": [
    "Letters Unicode assigned after version 15.0 that the name fold changes, with the letter each folds to under Unicode 17.0: capitals lowercased, and letters whose compatibility form (NFKC) is an ASCII letter or digit, as the outlined Latin letters are. Those also carry `compatibility`, their NFKC form under Unicode 17.0, which keeps their case: the camel-case reading (markWordSteps) runs on text before it is lowercased.",
    "Why: each engine folds by the Unicode version it ships. Production's Postgres lowercases with ICU at Unicode 15.1 and normalizes with Postgres's own tables, while Node may know Unicode 17.0, so text such a letter spells was folded by one engine and not the other: the erasure and the digest rule read the same name differently. The fold reads these letters from this list first (services/memory/person-name-characters.ts, foldName; services/memory/person-erasure.ts, foldedText), so it no longer depends on any engine's version for them.",
    "Complete from Unicode 15.0 through `through`: every code point assigned in 15.1, 16.0 or 17.0 whose fold is not itself (15.1 added none), found by comparing the code points each version assigns. services/memory/erasure-rule-agreement.realdb.test.ts checks each entry against the running JavaScript, and its every-character sweep fails, naming the code point, when an engine knows a newer letter that is not here.",
    "Each entry is one code point folding to one code point, as Postgres's translate() needs. The graph (memory-service/app/name_fold.py) does not read this list: Neo4j folds these letters as its Java version does."
  ],
